# Review standard for the writing revision of the released paper (objective #6)

Owner: `internal-review-repo` (function: review). The assembled delivery for the
`Research objective` #6 is judged against this standard before approval. The
standard for objective #5 (the extension, released at paper `771f5a90e56b`) is
kept as history in `REVIEW_STANDARD_obj5.md`.

## Sources

- **Objective #6, re-read at every verdict.** It asks for the writing of the
  released paper and only the writing. It names the structure and order of the
  argument, how each claim is stated and supported, the wording, how figures
  and tables are introduced and interpreted, the captions, and whether each
  citation sits in a sentence about the cited work. Where the text claims more
  or less than the evidence in the repositories shows, the text changes to match
  the evidence. There are no new experiments, no changed results or conclusions,
  and no new research directions. Figures and tables may be redrawn or laid out
  again for readability, from existing results only. The delivery is the LaTeX
  source in the ICLR 2027 template and the compiled PDF, within the submission
  requirements and the main-text page limit, with the appendices kept as
  supplementary material. A record of what was changed and why sits next to the
  paper. Nothing first published after 18 Oct 2026 is used.
- **The baseline: the released delivery.** It consists of paper-repo `771f5a90e56b`,
  figtab-repo `924ea5af4d20` and experiment-repo `4207193fc02e`. I approved it
  for objective #5; see `reviews/release_review.md` and `reviews/log.md`. Its
  numbers, registered verdicts and conclusions are the fixed reference for the
  invariance checks (I below).
- **The brief** (`ht-role brief`): v5.0 at idea-repo `dcecc0510060`, written for the writing objective.
  - Its requirements W1–W7, K1–K7 and D1–D5 map onto W, I and B below.
  - Its diagnosis (§4, items 1–15) and its claim-versus-evidence candidates (§5, items 1–8) are checked one by one at each verdict, together with experiment-repo's evidence check in paper-repo#16 (items 1–9).
  - Its citation audit (`refs/citation_audit.md`, promised as v5.1) is checked against my own sample once it is pushed.
- **The work assignment** at story-repo `84409bbd`:
  - paper-repo owns `paper/`;
  - figtab-repo owns `paper/figures/`, `paper/tables/` and `code/figures/`;
  - experiment-repo owns `code/` and `results/`.
- **venue-repo's ICLR 2027 record** at `1f0048accdba`. Its section "Objective venue-repo#10" covers this revision: `VENUE.md`,
  `template/`, `tools/check_submission.py` and `venue-artifacts.json`.
  - Main text: at most 9 pages. References do not count.
  - Appendices go after the references, are unlimited, and are optional reading.
    The paper must stand alone without them.
  - The submission is anonymous, with no acknowledgments.
  - An AI use statement is required. Reproducibility and ethics statements are
    recommended.
  - `paper/CHANGES.md` and `paper/CLAIMS.md` are required paths.
    - CHANGES.md carries a section for this revision.
    - CLAIMS.md stays consistent with the text.

This standard never prescribes a conclusion or a wording. I will revise it,
and record why, if the brief, the assignment or the venue record changes what
#6 is read to require.

## W. Writing: what a demanding referee checks

- **W1. Argument and order.**
  - The abstract states the problem, why it is hard, the idea, the headline
    results and their scope, and the significance.
  - The introduction motivates with the concrete gap. It states the idea and
    lists contributions that are each verifiable in the paper, with their scope
    (linear model, ViT).
  - The order of sections follows the argument: setup, theory, predictions,
    tests, extensions, limits.
  - The premise ("collapse is decided early") and the evidence against it in the
    ViT are framed consistently from the abstract to the conclusion. A reader
    must not meet a premise in the introduction that the abstract or Section 7
    contradicts without warning.
- **W2. Claims stated and supported.**
  - Every claim in the abstract, the contributions and the conclusion maps to a
    section, figure or table and to a results file, with its scope and its
    verdict (pass, fail, post hoc, retrospective, undetermined).
  - No claim is stronger than its evidence, for example generalising beyond the
    linear model, or treating "not separated" as "better".
  - No claim is weaker than its evidence, for example burying a passed held-out
    test.
  - Each registered failure stays stated.
- **W3. Wording.**
  - Every term the main text uses is defined in the main text at first use.
  - Internal process labels (rung n, v3/v4, A2′, B1′, N2, A1, E-numbers, "the
    word", "truth ill-posed", "sample-robust") either get a plain-language
    definition or are replaced by a description. The appendix may keep the
    labels if it maps them.
  - Sentences carry one claim each where possible. There are no unexplained
    numbers or symbols, no filler, and no paragraph repeated across sections.
- **W4. Figures and tables.**
  - Each one is referenced in the text, introduced before it is needed, and
    interpreted: the text says what to see and what it means.
  - Each caption stands alone. It states what is plotted, n, seeds, the
    statistic and the interval, and the claim the item supports.
  - Labels and legends are legible at ICLR text width in print, about 6 pt or
    more, with no internal jargon in panel titles.
  - Colour use is consistent and colourblind-safe.
  - A figure the main-text argument depends on is in the main text, or the text
    says it is in the appendix and the claim can be read without it.
  - No figure or table is blank, a placeholder, or unlabeled.
- **W5. Citations.**
  - Each `\cite` sits in a sentence about what the cited work does, and the
    claim is true of the work (I check it against the title or abstract).
  - No bundle of more than 4 keys, unless every key supports the sentence.
  - No key dumps or citation-only sentences.
  - Relevant entries are not left uncited.
  - Every key resolves, and nothing first published after 18 Oct 2026 is used.
  - The related work is thematic and says how this paper differs.
- **W6. Limitations and conclusion.**
  - They are candid and specific: what holds, where it fails, and what is open.
  - They make no new claims.
- **W7. Stand-alone main text.** The main text can be read without the
  appendix. Its claims do not rest only on tables or figures that the reader
  must find in the appendix. Cross-references to the appendix name what is
  there.

## I. Invariance: only the writing changes

- **I1. results/ and code/.** At the delivered experiment-repo revision they are
  unchanged against `4207193`, or changed only in documentation such as the
  README. I diff the two trees.
- **I2. Same numbers, same sources.** Every number in the revised text equals a
  value in the released text or in a delivered results file. `numbers.tex`
  regenerates from `results/`. I diff the set of macros and values against
  `771f5a9` and explain every added or changed macro by a results file.
- **I3. Same verdicts and conclusions.** Every registered verdict (pass, fail,
  undetermined, post hoc, retrospective) and every conclusion of the released
  text is unchanged. It may be reworded, but not reversed, dropped or
  strengthened.
- **I4. Redrawn figures and tables.** Each one is regenerated by
  `code/figures/` from existing results files only. I re-run the generation for
  each changed item.
- **I5. Bibliography.** No new reference is first published after 18 Oct 2026.
  A new citation must be in the bibliography with a verified date.

## B. Build and venue

- **B1.** I build the committed source as committed, and a second copy with
  `numbers.tex` regenerated from the delivery root. The committed PDF matches
  both (pdftotext).
- **B2.** The build has 0 undefined references or citations, no "??", and no
  TODO.
- **B3.** venue-repo's `tools/check_submission.py` passes. I look at the
  rendered pages to confirm the 9-page main text, the anonymity, and the
  statements.
- **B4.** The appendices follow the references, as supplementary material.
- **B5.** The change record next to the paper says what changed and why. venue-repo
  requires it as a section of `paper/CHANGES.md`; paper-repo plans `paper/REVISION.md`,
  which needs a pointer from CHANGES.md. I check it against the actual diff from `771f5a9`, both ways.

## How a verdict is made

- I read the whole main text of the delivered PDF as a referee. I open every
  main-text page and every figure and table. I read the appendix sections the
  main text points to.
- I record what I inspected, at which revisions, and the outcome per criterion
  in `reviews/log.md`.
- Every open actionable finding goes in one pass to its owner as a `revise`
  decision:
  - findings on the paper go to paper-repo;
  - findings on figures and tables go to figtab-repo, through paper-repo;
  - findings on results documentation go to experiment-repo.
- I approve with `ht-role approve` only when W, I and B pass at the exact
  delivered revisions.
